small businesses and their employees  have paid a  heavy price during the pandemic there  are nearly  32 million small businesses in the u.s  employing  more than 44 47 percent of workers the  pandemic has forced  more than 400 thousand to close the wall  street firm goldman sachs is  installing ten thousand help still  wanted signs  on capitol hill they represent  businesses that graduated  from their ten thousand small business  program over the past  10 years and show that government  assistance is still needed goldman sachs  chairman and ceo  david solomon is in washington to lobby  lawmakers on behalf of small businesses  and joins us now good morning mr solomon  thanks so much for being here  we really appreciate it thank you very  much for having me i'm thrilled to be  with you this morning  i want to get to small business in a  second but i want to ask you about a  letter that  that you signed yesterday you were one  of 200 business leaders who signed it  calling on congress to accept the  electoral college results in that letter  you wrote that attempts to thwart or  delay this process run counter to the  essential  tenets of our democracy there should be  no further delay in the orderly transfer  of power  what made you sign on to that  well i think i think right now the  discussions around  moving forward are very very important  we i congratulated the vice the vice  president-elect now the president-elect  back in november after the election and  with all the issues we have as we're  trying to move past the pandemic  and move forward to help people who need  help around the country i think it's  time for us to move forward  and get focused on the issues at hand we  uh there is another round of of stimulus  money coming to small businesses  uh but but uh many of them ninety  percent  uh have already used up the money they  got from the first round they're in dire  straits  how are small businesses surviving and  what more needs to be done at this point  well it's really amazing to see how so  many small businesses are being nimble  trying to pivot trying to find ways to  move forward but one of the reasons  we're here today and we're so committed  to the 10  000 small business program is these  businesses continue to need help  and if you look at the placards that are  across the capital lawn this morning  they talk about a number of issues that  small businesses still need support and  help on obviously this additional round  of ppp funding is very helpful obviously  making sure that the ppp loans don't  have a negative tax impact  is helpful but i'll highlight three  other things that the small businesses  that we've helped over the last decade  are asking for first better access to  capital and something like the restart  act i think can be very helpful with  that  they need better access for their  workers to health care because health  care can be such a crippling cost  and in addition they also need support  with  child care because they need to be  working they need to be focused on their  businesses so they can keep their  businesses moving forward so those are  just a handful of the things  that small businesses are telling us  they need continued support on given the  pressure they've felt  during this pandemic mr solomon i want  to talk about your desire to help  specifically black business owners your  own survey has showed that they have a  harder time getting the business loans  and what needs to be done to fix that i  understand that you want to do something  about that specifically  we've we've tried to find ways to get  capital  into the hands of business of color it's  been much more difficult  for small entrepreneurial businesses of  color to access capital gail  and in the context of that we through  ppp deployed about 700  million dollars of capital and with this  new ppp round  through cdfis and mission-driven lenders  we're prepared to commit at least  another 500 million dollars of capital  to get that capital access to these  small businesses  and these are small micro loans that can  help these small businesses  bridge through over the course of the  coming months until we get to the other  side of the pandemic  you talk about the importance of getting  to the other side how how critical is a  vaccine to the economy mr solomon do you  think  i i think it's i think it's very  critical that we get the vaccine  into people so that people feel safe and  we can move forward  with the economic vitality that's so  important to this country so  i'm watching very closely as you all are  as we all are  it's important for us to get to the  other side of the pandemic and the  vaccine is an important step  in that process yeah and and your  additional commitment that you've  announced today  amounts to how much well we we committed  back in december we announced that we  commit another 250  million dollars to the 10 000 small  business program so that we could bring  another  10 000 businesses through it in the  coming years  and in addition now that the new round  of ppp is in place  we're prepared to continue through cdfis  and mission-driven lenders  to deploy another 500 million dollars of  capital in small loans to help these  businesses sustain  david solomon of goldman sachs thanks so  much for being with us we appreciate it
 

